sistently > logprob on NER ρ across all 4 model×dataset configs\n\nTable 5 (Best-of-N selection, SciERC, N=16):\n- NER: greedy 0.618, logprob 0.621(+0.003), SJ 0.620(+0.002), oracle 0.787(+0.169)\n- RE: greedy 0.403, logprob 0.410(+0.007), SJ 0.397(-0.006), oracle 0.631(+0.228)\n\nTable 6 (N-scaling): N=16 SJ ρ NER mean=0.412±0.025 (3 seeds), RE mean=0.271±0.006\n\nTable 7 (Ensemble): Logistic ensemble NER AUROC=0.735, RE AUROC=0.719; ensemble still cannot beat greedy on NER selection (0.604 < 0.609)\n\nTable 8 (Per-relation RE): HYPONYM-OF ρ_SJ=0.545, FEATURE-OF ρ_SJ=0.004\n\nTable 9 (OOD, Appendix): SJ degrades 0.357→0.15, logprob inverts 0.140→-0.20\n\nSection 5.8 (Temperature): SJ robust Δρ=±0.01 between T=0.7 and T=1.0\n\nSection 5.9 (Capability stratification): SJ strongest on simple instances (entity count=0: ρ=0.689), degrades on complex (count 3-5: ρ=0.353). Hard RE (F1<0.25): SJ ρ=-0.043\n\nSection 6: Decision flowchart — use structural consistency for selective prediction/abstention; use logprob for best-of-N selection where greedy is suboptimal; for closed-source APIs, structural consistency is the only option.\n\nFigure 1: Scatter plot showing correlation-selection gap on SciERC NER\nFigure 2: Best-of-N selection curves showing all signals plateau near greedy while oracle grows\n\n**Experimental design reference (imagined experiments list)**:\nThe following experiments were planned. Assess coverage:\n- baseline_logprob (exp-012): SJ vs logprob comparison ✓ covered in Table 1\n- baseline_exact_match (exp-013): EM baseline ✓ covered\n- baseline_entity_voting (exp-014): Entity voting ✓ covered\n- evaluation_metrics_expansion (exp-015): Unified 5-signal evaluation ✓ covered in Table 1\n- ner_conll2003 (exp-002): Cross-dataset ✓ covered in Table 2\n- ner_wnut17 (exp-003): WNUT-17, expected FAIL → mentioned as abandoned in Section 4\n- ner_multi_model (exp-007): LLaMA on SciERC ✓ covered in Table 3\n- ner_llama_conll (exp-017): LLaMA on CoNLL for 2×2 matrix ✓ covered in Table 4\n- ner_n16_scierc (exp-001): N=16 + 3-seed ✓ covered in Table 6\n- adaptive_compute_demo (exp-006): Best-of-N selection ✓ covered in Table 5\n- signal_ensemble_analysis (exp-016): Ensemble ✓ covered in Table 7\n- selection_curves (exp-010): Selection curves ✓ covered in Figure 2\n- re_diagnostic_analysis (exp-004): Per-relation breakdown ✓ covered in Table 8\n- temperature_sensitivity_analysis (exp-005): Temperature ✓ covered in Section 5.8\n- capability_stratification (exp-009): Difficulty stratification ✓ covered in Section 5.9\n- re_n16_confirmation (exp-008): RE N=16 → partially covered in Table 6\n- ner_ood_robustness (exp-011): OOD ✓ covered in Table 9 (Appendix)\n\n**Your review should cover**:\n1. **Soundness** (1-5): Are the methods correct? Is the evaluation rigorous? Any flaws in experimental design?\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, labeled W1, W2, etc.)\n4. **Questions for authors** (Q1, Q2, etc.)\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**\n6. **Minor issues**\n7. **Overall assessment** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous and fair. Note both strengths and weaknesses. Think about what a critical but fair reviewer at a top venue would flag.","uuid":"7f459166-0221-41ad-97b6-1b5efaad758a","session_id":"3a0e666b-279a-4720-93f0-85a9681f76b3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Review the following paper and produce a structured review.\n\n**Paper**: \"Structural Consistency for Confidence Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison and the Correlation-Selection Gap\"\n\n**Paper Summary**:\nThe paper systematically compares 5 unsupervised confidence signals for LLM-based information extraction (NER and RE): (1) sequence log-probability, (2) exact match rate, (3) Fleiss' Kappa, (4) soft Jaccard similarity (SJ), and (5) entity-level majority voting confidence. Evaluated on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Structural consistency signals (voting, SJ) are the strongest quality estimators for IE, outperforming logprob (ΔAUROC up to +0.17).\n2. A \"correlation-selection gap\" exists: strong instance-level quality estimation (ρ≈0.50) does not translate to effective best-of-N selection — no signal beats greedy decoding on NER.\n3. Compound error propagation in relational tasks degrades all signals, with per-relation-type heterogeneity (ρ=0.545 on HYPONYM-OF to ρ=0.004 on FEATURE-OF).\n\n**Key experimental details from the paper**:\n\nTable 1 (SciERC, N=8, T=1.0):\n- NER: voting_conf ρ=0.502/AUROC=0.728, SJ ρ=0.357/AUROC=0.666, FK ρ=0.265/AUROC=0.624, EM ρ=0.257/AUROC=0.623, logprob ρ=0.140/AUROC=0.559\n- RE: voting_conf ρ=0.422/AUROC=0.716, FK ρ=0.253/AUROC=0.631, logprob ρ=0.247/AUROC=0.635, SJ ρ=0.211/AUROC=0.582, EM ρ=0.100/AUROC=0.504\n\nTable 2 (CoNLL-2003 NER, Qwen3-8B, N=8):\n- EM ρ=0.451, SJ ρ=0.436, voting_conf ρ=0.424, FK ρ=0.400, logprob ρ=0.316\n\nTable 3 (LLaMA-3.1-8B, SciERC): NER SJ ρ=0.363 vs logprob 0.183; RE SJ ρ=0.299 vs logprob 0.288\n\nTable 4 (2×2 matrix): SJ consistently > logprob on NER ρ across all 4 model×dataset configs\n\nTable 5 (Best-of-N selection, SciERC, N=16):\n- NER: greedy 0.618, logprob 0.621(+0.003), SJ 0.620(+0.002), oracle 0.787(+0.169)\n- RE: greedy 0.403, logprob 0.410(+0.007), SJ 0.397(-0.006), oracle 0.631(+0.228)\n\nTable 6 (N-scaling): N=16 SJ ρ NER mean=0.412±0.025 (3 seeds), RE mean=0.271±0.006\n\nTable 7 (Ensemble): Logistic ensemble NER AUROC=0.735, RE AUROC=0.719; ensemble still cannot beat greedy on NER selection (0.604 < 0.609)\n\nTable 8 (Per-relation RE): HYPONYM-OF ρ_SJ=0.545, FEATURE-OF ρ_SJ=0.004\n\nTable 9 (OOD, Appendix): SJ degrades 0.357→0.15, logprob inverts 0.140→-0.20\n\nSection 5.8 (Temperature): SJ robust Δρ=±0.01 between T=0.7 and T=1.0\n\nSection 5.9 (Capability stratification): SJ strongest on simple instances (entity count=0: ρ=0.689), degrades on complex (count 3-5: ρ=0.353). Hard RE (F1<0.25): SJ ρ=-0.043\n\nSection 6: Decision flowchart — use structural consistency for selective prediction/abstention; use logprob for best-of-N selection where greedy is suboptimal; for closed-source APIs, structural consistency is the only option.\n\nFigure 1: Scatter plot showing correlation-selection gap on SciERC NER\nFigure 2: Best-of-N selection curves showing all signals plateau near greedy while oracle grows\n\n**Experimental design reference (imagined experiments list)**:\nThe following experiments were planned. Assess coverage:\n- baseline_logprob (exp-012): SJ vs logprob comparison ✓ covered in Table 1\n- baseline_exact_match (exp-013): EM baseline ✓ covered\n- baseline_entity_voting (exp-014): Entity voting ✓ covered\n- evaluation_metrics_expansion (exp-015): Unified 5-signal evaluation ✓ covered in Table 1\n- ner_conll2003 (exp-002): Cross-dataset ✓ covered in Table 2\n- ner_wnut17 (exp-003): WNUT-17, expected FAIL → mentioned as abandoned in Section 4\n- ner_multi_model (exp-007): LLaMA on SciERC ✓ covered in Table 3\n- ner_llama_conll (exp-017): LLaMA on CoNLL for 2×2 matrix ✓ covered in Table 4\n- ner_n16_scierc (exp-001): N=16 + 3-seed ✓ covered in Table 6\n- adaptive_compute_demo (exp-006): Best-of-N selection ✓ covered in Table 5\n- signal_ensemble_analysis (exp-016): Ensemble ✓ covered in Table 7\n- selection_curves (exp-010): Selection curves ✓ covered in Figure 2\n- re_diagnostic_analysis (exp-004): Per-relation breakdown ✓ covered in Table 8\n- temperature_sensitivity_analysis (exp-005): Temperature ✓ covered in Section 5.8\n- capability_stratification (exp-009): Difficulty stratification ✓ covered in Section 5.9\n- re_n16_confirmation (exp-008): RE N=16 → partially covered in Table 6\n- ner_ood_robustness (exp-011): OOD ✓ covered in Table 9 (Appendix)\n\n**Your review should cover**:\n1. **Soundness** (1-5): Are the methods correct? Is the evaluation rigorous? Any flaws in experimental design?\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n3. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, labeled W1, W2, etc.)\n4. **Questions for authors** (Q1, Q2, etc.)\n5. **Missing experiments or analyses**\n6. **Minor issues**\n7. **Overall assessment** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n8. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous and fair. Note both strengths and weaknesses. Think about what a critical but fair reviewer at a top venue would flag."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_0186jz8tE7sFwudQJZHCY5LU","session_id":"3a0e666b-279a-4720-93f0-85a9681f76b3","uuid":"5a5bbbb1-8f76-4b7b-b89a-1697850e6f51","timestamp":"2026-05-14T21:25:01.514Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FZmBnh5xyjhR9a51FBrxEA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011Kr8BxB7iwcqg4jHEsyXw5","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. Review the following paper and produce a structured review.\n\n**Paper**: \"Structural Consistency for Confidence Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison and the Correlation-Selection Gap\"\n\n**Paper Summary**:\nThe paper systematically compares 5 unsupervised confidence signals for LLM-based information extraction (NER and RE): (1) sequence log-probability, (2) exact match rate, (3) Fleiss' Kappa, (4) soft Jaccard similarity (SJ), and (5) entity-level majority voting confidence. Evaluated on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 with Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B.\n\nKey claims:\n1. Structural consistency signals (voting, SJ) are the strongest quality estimators for IE, outperforming logprob (ΔAUROC up to +0.17).\n2. A \"correlation-selection gap\": strong instance-level quality estimation (ρ≈0.50) does not translate to effective best-of-N selection — no signal beats greedy decoding on NER despite +16pp oracle headroom.\n3. Compound error propagation in RE degrades all signals, with per-relation-type heterogeneity.\n\n**Contributions as stated**:\n1. First systematic empirical comparison of 5 unsupervised confidence signals across 4 families for LLM-based IE. Contribution is in the taxonomy and comparative methodology, not any individual signal.\n2. Identification of the correlation-selection gap. Practical decision flowchart (Section 6).\n3. Empirical analysis of task-complexity boundaries.\n\n**Related work covered** (Section 2):\n- Uncertainty Quantification for LLMs: Geng et al. 2025, Hou et al. 2025, Xiong et al. 2024, Yoshikawa et al. 2025; token-level logprobs (Kadavath et al. 2022), semantic entropy (Kuhn et al. 2023), probes (Kossen et al. 2024), SelfCheckGPT (Manakul et al. 2023); calibration (Guo et al. 2017, Desai & Durrett 2020, Kuleshov & Liang 2015)\n- Self-Consistency and TTS: Wang et al. 2023, CISC (Huang et al. 2025), Self-Certainty Best-of-N (Zhang et al. 2026), ReASC (Li et al. 2026), Atomic Self-Consistency (Chen et al. 2024), Ranked Voting (Liu et al. 2025), Latent Self-Consistency (Wu et al. 2025); scaling laws (Snell et al. 2024, Brown et al. 2024); verifiers (Cobbe et al. 2021, Lightman et al. 2023)\n- Confidence for Structured IE: Nédellec & Xia 2026 (surface-level string matching, acknowledged limitation); Culotta & McCallum 2004 (CRF-based); Xiao & Wang 2019, Shelmanov et al. 2021; structured calibration (Jagannatha & Yu 2022); ensemble/MC-dropout (Lakshminarayanan et al. 2017, Gal & Ghahramani 2016); CoCoA (Vashurin et al. 2025a); selective prediction (Geifman & El-Yaniv 2017)\n\n**Key experimental results** (for context):\n- NER quality estimation: voting_conf AUROC=0.728, SJ AUROC=0.666, logprob AUROC=0.559 on SciERC\n- Best-of-N selection: No signal beats greedy on NER; only logprob marginally helps on RE (+0.7pp)\n- 2×2 cross-validation (2 models × 2 datasets): SJ consistently > logprob on NER ρ\n- RE per-relation heterogeneity: ρ from 0.545 to 0.004\n- OOD: structural signals degrade but remain positive; logprob inverts to negative correlation\n- Temperature: SJ robust (Δρ=±0.01), FK more sensitive (Δρ=±0.06)\n\n**Section 6 (Practical Guidance)**: Decision framework — when to use structural consistency (selective prediction, abstention, closed-source APIs) vs logprob (best-of-N selection on tasks where greedy is suboptimal) vs when neither helps (NER best-of-N with current models, tasks where model systematically fails).\n\n**Section 7 (Discussion)**: Argues correlation-selection gap may extend beyond IE. Discusses closed-source API applicability. Lists 5 limitations: (1) only 8B models, (2) only instruction-tuned, (3) only well-structured text, (4) RE only on SciERC, (5) no supervised baselines.\n\n**References**: 30 references total, spanning uncertainty quantification, self-consistency, structured prediction, calibration.\n\n**Your review should cover**:\n1. **Novelty** (1-5): How novel are the contributions? Does this paper advance the field meaningfully?\n2. **Significance** (1-5): How important are the findings for the community?\n3. **Related work completeness**: Are key prior works cited? Any missing comparisons?\n4. **Strengths** (bullet points)\n5. **Weaknesses** (bullet points, labeled W1, W2, etc.)\n6. **Questions for authors** (Q1, Q2, etc.)\n7. **Positioning concerns**: Does the paper overclaim or underclaim?\n8. **Minor issues**\n9. **Overall assessment** (1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept)\n10. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous and fair. Think about what a novelty-focused reviewer at a top venue would flag. Consider: Is \"systematic comparison\" enough for a top venue? Is the correlation-selection gap truly novel or expected? Are the individual signals themselves novel?","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14362,"cache_read_input_tokens":38239,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14362},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a0e666b-279a-4720-93f0-85a9681f76b3","uuid":"507c5877-7085-4c9d-a61e-566f4d7fe2ff"}
