{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KBs8Dxf9Ges7yGL7AFWda4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RuxfWwj2JrwcpfupNTXtUe","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Roundtable Steps 2-3: Cross-sharing and convergence","prompt":"You are the roundtable moderator for a top ML venue peer review. Two reviewers have independently reviewed the paper \"Structural Consistency for Confidence Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison and the Correlation-Selection Gap.\" Your job is to synthesize the cross-sharing (Step 2) and convergence (Step 3) phases.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Soundness: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Comprehensive signal comparison under unified evaluation protocol\n- Correlation-selection gap is genuine and underappreciated\n- Per-relation heterogeneity (Table 8) is the most diagnostic finding\n- OOD analysis showing logprob sign inversion is important\n- Decision flowchart is practically useful\n- Seed-level variance reporting adds credibility\n\n**Key Weaknesses (W1-W8):**\n- W1: Best-of-N oracle gap not explained mechanistically — why does ρ≈0.50 fail at selection?\n- W2: N=8/T=1.0 primary configuration not justified; practitioner regime may be N=2 or N=4\n- W3: CoNLL-2003 coverage asymmetric (no LLaMA×CoNLL in main tables, no CoNLL RE)\n- W4: WNUT-17 abandonment unacknowledged in claim scope\n- W5: SciERC label quality not discussed; FEATURE-OF ρ=0.004 could be annotation noise\n- W6: Ensemble analysis lacks feature importance ablation\n- W7: Confidence intervals missing in main tables (ρ point estimates only)\n- W8: No comparison to calibration baselines (temperature scaling, Platt scaling on logprob)\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- Q1: Top-1 accuracy of each signal for selection — fraction of time signal's best > greedy?\n- Q2: WNUT-17 failure details?\n- Q3: FEATURE-OF near-zero ρ — annotation noise or signal collapse?\n- Q5: OOD setup details — which dataset, what kind of shift?\n- Q6: Temperature below 0.5?\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Score: 3 (Weak Reject), Novelty: 2/5, Significance: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Clean experimental setup, reproducible\n- Per-relation heterogeneity genuinely informative\n- OOD logprob inversion is the most novel empirical finding\n- Temperature sensitivity analysis methodologically useful\n- Decision flowchart synthesizes findings into actionable guidance\n- 2×2 cross-validation strengthens structural > logprob claim\n\n**Key Weaknesses (W1-W7):**\n- W1: Scope insufficient — 2 datasets, 2 models, 1 task family, 5 pre-existing signals is narrow for a \"systematic comparison\"\n- W2: Correlation-selection gap is reframed prior knowledge (Geifman & El-Yaniv 2017, selective prediction literature)\n- W3: Best-of-N NER failure underanalyzed — stops at documenting, no mechanism\n- W4: Only 8B instruction-tuned models; logprob behavior differs substantially for base/larger models\n- W5: SJ sensitivity to parsing errors not characterized\n- W6: Statistical significance underreported throughout\n- W7: \"First systematic comparison\" claim needs qualification\n\n**Positioning Concerns:**\n- Overclaiming: \"first systematic comparison\" too strong given prior surveys; \"correlation-selection gap\" presented as discovery vs. domain-specific documentation of known effect\n- Underclaiming: OOD logprob inversion and per-relation heterogeneity deserve stronger emphasis\n\n**Missing Related Work:**\n- Conformal prediction for structured outputs\n- Prompt robustness for IE literature\n- CISC overlap not clearly differentiated\n- Structured prediction uncertainty (CRF marginals etc.) underengaged\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where the two reviewers agree and disagree. Note:\n1. Points of strong agreement\n2. Points of disagreement or differing emphasis\n3. Issues raised by only one reviewer that the other should consider\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nFor each major issue, determine:\n1. Is this a **fatal flaw** (would block acceptance at any top venue)?\n2. Is this a **major weakness** (significant but addressable in revision)?\n3. Is this a **minor weakness** (noted but not acceptance-blocking)?\n\nThen produce a **converged assessment** with:\n- Agreed strengths (ranked)\n- Agreed weaknesses (ranked by severity)\n- Converged score recommendation (1-6 scale)\n- Key conditions for acceptance (if borderline)\n\nBe fair and calibrated. A score of 3-4 at a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate means this paper is in the borderline zone.","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11223,"cache_read_input_tokens":52601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11223},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a0e666b-279a-4720-93f0-85a9681f76b3","uuid":"1ba2e74f-05de-45c3-8b62-928e45df6aad"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a764f261c3e22cbf0","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RuxfWwj2JrwcpfupNTXtUe","description":"Roundtable Steps 2-3: Cross-sharing and convergence","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are the roundtable moderator for a top ML venue peer review. Two reviewers have independently reviewed the paper \"Structural Consistency for Confidence Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison and the Correlation-Selection Gap.\" Your job is to synthesize the cross-sharing (Step 2) and convergence (Step 3) phases.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Soundness: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Comprehensive signal comparison under unified evaluation protocol\n- Correlation-selection gap is genuine and underappreciated\n- Per-relation heterogeneity (Table 8) is the most diagnostic finding\n- OOD analysis showing logprob sign inversion is important\n- Decision flowchart is practically useful\n- Seed-level variance reporting adds credibility\n\n**Key Weaknesses (W1-W8):**\n- W1: Best-of-N oracle gap not explained mechanistically — why does ρ≈0.50 fail at selection?\n- W2: N=8/T=1.0 primary configuration not justified; practitioner regime may be N=2 or N=4\n- W3: CoNLL-2003 coverage asymmetric (no LLaMA×CoNLL in main tables, no CoNLL RE)\n- W4: WNUT-17 abandonment unacknowledged in claim scope\n- W5: SciERC label quality not discussed; FEATURE-OF ρ=0.004 could be annotation noise\n- W6: Ensemble analysis lacks feature importance ablation\n- W7: Confidence intervals missing in main tables (ρ point estimates only)\n- W8: No comparison to calibration baselines (temperature scaling, Platt scaling on logprob)\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- Q1: Top-1 accuracy of each signal for selection — fraction of time signal's best > greedy?\n- Q2: WNUT-17 failure details?\n- Q3: FEATURE-OF near-zero ρ — annotation noise or signal collapse?\n- Q5: OOD setup details — which dataset, what kind of shift?\n- Q6: Temperature below 0.5?\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Score: 3 (Weak Reject), Novelty: 2/5, Significance: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Clean experimental setup, reproducible\n- Per-relation heterogeneity genuinely informative\n- OOD logprob inversion is the most novel empirical finding\n- Temperature sensitivity analysis methodologically useful\n- Decision flowchart synthesizes findings into actionable guidance\n- 2×2 cross-validation strengthens structural > logprob claim\n\n**Key Weaknesses (W1-W7):**\n- W1: Scope insufficient — 2 datasets, 2 models, 1 task family, 5 pre-existing signals is narrow for a \"systematic comparison\"\n- W2: Correlation-selection gap is reframed prior knowledge (Geifman & El-Yaniv 2017, selective prediction literature)\n- W3: Best-of-N NER failure underanalyzed — stops at documenting, no mechanism\n- W4: Only 8B instruction-tuned models; logprob behavior differs substantially for base/larger models\n- W5: SJ sensitivity to parsing errors not characterized\n- W6: Statistical significance underreported throughout\n- W7: \"First systematic comparison\" claim needs qualification\n\n**Positioning Concerns:**\n- Overclaiming: \"first systematic comparison\" too strong given prior surveys; \"correlation-selection gap\" presented as discovery vs. domain-specific documentation of known effect\n- Underclaiming: OOD logprob inversion and per-relation heterogeneity deserve stronger emphasis\n\n**Missing Related Work:**\n- Conformal prediction for structured outputs\n- Prompt robustness for IE literature\n- CISC overlap not clearly differentiated\n- Structured prediction uncertainty (CRF marginals etc.) underengaged\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where the two reviewers agree and disagree. Note:\n1. Points of strong agreement\n2. Points of disagreement or differing emphasis\n3. Issues raised by only one reviewer that the other should consider\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nFor each major issue, determine:\n1. Is this a **fatal flaw** (would block acceptance at any top venue)?\n2. Is this a **major weakness** (significant but addressable in revision)?\n3. Is this a **minor weakness** (noted but not acceptance-blocking)?\n\nThen produce a **converged assessment** with:\n- Agreed strengths (ranked)\n- Agreed weaknesses (ranked by severity)\n- Converged score recommendation (1-6 scale)\n- Key conditions for acceptance (if borderline)\n\nBe fair and calibrated. A score of 3-4 at a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate means this paper is in the borderline zone.","uuid":"e3e75ffb-b8a5-4095-99cf-9846cf091db3","session_id":"3a0e666b-279a-4720-93f0-85a9681f76b3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are the roundtable moderator for a top ML venue peer review. Two reviewers have independently reviewed the paper \"Structural Consistency for Confidence Estimation in LLM-Based Information Extraction: A Systematic Comparison and the Correlation-Selection Gap.\" Your job is to synthesize the cross-sharing (Step 2) and convergence (Step 3) phases.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Score: 4 (Weak Accept), Soundness: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Comprehensive signal comparison under unified evaluation protocol\n- Correlation-selection gap is genuine and underappreciated\n- Per-relation heterogeneity (Table 8) is the most diagnostic finding\n- OOD analysis showing logprob sign inversion is important\n- Decision flowchart is practically useful\n- Seed-level variance reporting adds credibility\n\n**Key Weaknesses (W1-W8):**\n- W1: Best-of-N oracle gap not explained mechanistically — why does ρ≈0.50 fail at selection?\n- W2: N=8/T=1.0 primary configuration not justified; practitioner regime may be N=2 or N=4\n- W3: CoNLL-2003 coverage asymmetric (no LLaMA×CoNLL in main tables, no CoNLL RE)\n- W4: WNUT-17 abandonment unacknowledged in claim scope\n- W5: SciERC label quality not discussed; FEATURE-OF ρ=0.004 could be annotation noise\n- W6: Ensemble analysis lacks feature importance ablation\n- W7: Confidence intervals missing in main tables (ρ point estimates only)\n- W8: No comparison to calibration baselines (temperature scaling, Platt scaling on logprob)\n\n**Key Questions:**\n- Q1: Top-1 accuracy of each signal for selection — fraction of time signal's best > greedy?\n- Q2: WNUT-17 failure details?\n- Q3: FEATURE-OF near-zero ρ — annotation noise or signal collapse?\n- Q5: OOD setup details — which dataset, what kind of shift?\n- Q6: Temperature below 0.5?\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work) — Score: 3 (Weak Reject), Novelty: 2/5, Significance: 3/5, Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Strengths:**\n- Clean experimental setup, reproducible\n- Per-relation heterogeneity genuinely informative\n- OOD logprob inversion is the most novel empirical finding\n- Temperature sensitivity analysis methodologically useful\n- Decision flowchart synthesizes findings into actionable guidance\n- 2×2 cross-validation strengthens structural > logprob claim\n\n**Key Weaknesses (W1-W7):**\n- W1: Scope insufficient — 2 datasets, 2 models, 1 task family, 5 pre-existing signals is narrow for a \"systematic comparison\"\n- W2: Correlation-selection gap is reframed prior knowledge (Geifman & El-Yaniv 2017, selective prediction literature)\n- W3: Best-of-N NER failure underanalyzed — stops at documenting, no mechanism\n- W4: Only 8B instruction-tuned models; logprob behavior differs substantially for base/larger models\n- W5: SJ sensitivity to parsing errors not characterized\n- W6: Statistical significance underreported throughout\n- W7: \"First systematic comparison\" claim needs qualification\n\n**Positioning Concerns:**\n- Overclaiming: \"first systematic comparison\" too strong given prior surveys; \"correlation-selection gap\" presented as discovery vs. domain-specific documentation of known effect\n- Underclaiming: OOD logprob inversion and per-relation heterogeneity deserve stronger emphasis\n\n**Missing Related Work:**\n- Conformal prediction for structured outputs\n- Prompt robustness for IE literature\n- CISC overlap not clearly differentiated\n- Structured prediction uncertainty (CRF marginals etc.) underengaged\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\nIdentify where the two reviewers agree and disagree. Note:\n1. Points of strong agreement\n2. Points of disagreement or differing emphasis\n3. Issues raised by only one reviewer that the other should consider\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\nFor each major issue, determine:\n1. Is this a **fatal flaw** (would block acceptance at any top venue)?\n2. Is this a **major weakness** (significant but addressable in revision)?\n3. Is this a **minor weakness** (noted but not acceptance-blocking)?\n\nThen produce a **converged assessment** with:\n- Agreed strengths (ranked)\n- Agreed weaknesses (ranked by severity)\n- Converged score recommendation (1-6 scale)\n- Key conditions for acceptance (if borderline)\n\nBe fair and calibrated. A score of 3-4 at a top venue with ~25% acceptance rate means this paper is in the borderline zone."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RuxfWwj2JrwcpfupNTXtUe","session_id":"3a0e666b-279a-4720-93f0-85a9681f76b3","uuid":"2f096290-eebf-4a07-aaa1-bda2232b82c4","timestamp":"2026-05-14T21:27:19.705Z"}
